SPU DMA: serve the SPU-thread local-store window (EA 0xF0000000 + n*0x100000 + LSA) from the LS of SPU thread n of the
issuing thread's group. Upstream candidate (general runtime fix; not yet proposed upstream).

Problem: lv2 maps, for every SPU thread of a thread group, the local store of each thread of THAT group at
0xF0000000 + n*0x100000 (LS at +0, problem state at +0x40000, SNR1 +0x5400C, SNR2 +0x5C00C; n = the thread number,
sys_spu_thread_initialize's thread_num; for SPURS, the SPU index of the instance). SPU code may GET/PUT a peer's LS
there. The runtime only has the raw-SPU window (0xE0000000, guest memory backed, spu_raw.c); an SPU thread's LS is the
host buffer ls_store, so a DMA to 0xF0000000+ went through the plain path to vm_base + 0xF0000000 (a demand-committed
zero page). Ratchet & Clank: Into the Nexus 1.01: the ig broadphase job writes its candidate items to its own LS 0x7380
and publishes cursor 0xF0000000 + 0x7380 (word [0xF3FF10 + 0x14] = 0xF0000000) in the narrow-queue line; the narrow job
GETs 16-byte items from that cursor and read zeros, so every collision query answered "no hit" and Ratchet sank through
the floor of prison_transport. RPCS3 serves the window for SPU threads (do_dma_transfer: thread index from the EA,
looked up in the issuer's group thread map; LS offsets redirect the copy to that thread's LS, out of range is an error;
a 4-byte PUT to SNR1/SNR2 signals the thread; other offsets are an error; read 2026-11-20 for behaviour, nothing
copied) [src:rpcs3-sputhread].

Fix:
- runtime/spu/spu_dma.h: mfc_do_transfer sends an element with EA >= 0xF0000000 to mfc_thread_ls_window after the size
  and LSA checks (plain GET/PUT and every GETL/PUTL element, which all come through it). n = (ea - 0xF0000000) >> 20,
  offset = ea & 0xFFFFF; LS offsets (< 0x40000, transfer inside the LS) are copied from/to the peer's ctx->ls (memmove:
  n may be the issuer). A GET also tells the overlay-residency tracker as a main-memory GET does. Problem-state offsets
  (sndsig to a peer SNR, which RPCS3 serves; register reads) and an issuer or slot the runtime does not know keep the
  old plain path and log once ([spu-tlsw] UNSUPPORTED / UNRESOLVED). SPU_TLSW_LOG=1 logs every served transfer (default: the first 4).
- runtime/spu/spu_channels.c: the group table spu_group_ls_bind / _unbind / _peer: (kind, key, index) -> context,
  kind lv2 (key = group id) or SPURS (key = instance EA), one slot per context, SRW lock, 128 slots. Lock-line atomics
  (GETLLAR/PUTLLC/PUTLLUC) into the window are not served from a peer (log once, old path): ASSUMPTION that no title
  needs them; the ig jobs use atomics on main memory only.
- runtime/spu/spurs_policy.c: spu_run_policy_module binds its context as SPU thread spu_num of the instance before the
  run (beside the 7034 SNR enter). The binding stays after the run: the SPU's LS keeps what the run left until another
  workload's context runs on that SPU.
- runtime/syscalls/lv2_register.c: group_start binds each lifted thread's context as thread t->index of the group;
  group_destroy unbinds before it frees the context.
The PPU has no mapping at 0xF0000000 on lv2 (it uses sys_spu_thread_read_ls/write_ls), so nothing changes on the PPU side.

Test: port/rc/tests/run_spu_thread_ls_window_test.sh (test_spu_thread_ls_window.c, real runtime link set, ASan+UBSan,
4 GB demand-commit guest space): lv2 group GET and PUT between two threads, a GETL with main-memory and window elements,
isolation between two groups, the SPURS hand-over through two real spu_run_policy_module runs on SPUs 0 and 1, the
plain path for an unbound context, problem state and after unbind. Negative control: the same test against the tree
with this patch reverse-applied fails cases 1-5.

